[38;5;231m⏺[39m Locked: server-issued at login, stubbed until there's a real backend.

  Next fork, and it's the one that's genuinely hard to walk back, because it determines both where the identifier lives
  and who can read it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m "persist" can mean three quite different lifetimes, and the choice also decides whether the id travels to
  your API automatically or only when code puts it there.

  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives tab close and browser restart, same-origin, readable only by your JS. Other forms read it
    explicitly and include it where they want it. Cost: no built-in expiry (you store a timestamp and enforce it
    yourself), and it's readable by any XSS on the origin, since JS-accessible storage can't be [38;5;153mHttpOnly[39m.
  - [1mCookie[0m — same persistence, plus it rides along on every request to the origin automatically, and gets a real
    [38;5;153mExpires[39m/[38;5;153mMax-Age[39m. Cost: it's on [3mevery[0m request whether or not that endpoint wants it, it enlarges the CSRF surface,
    and a cookie set from JS can't be [38;5;153mHttpOnly[39m either — so you pay the request-surface cost without the security win a
    server-set cookie would give you.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — per-tab, cleared when the tab closes. Cheapest and lowest-risk. But it doesn't survive a restart,
    which I read as failing your "it should persist."

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m.[0m Your stated consumer is other [3mforms[0m — client-side code that can read the value and
  put it in a payload deliberately. That doesn't need automatic transmission, and cookies charge you for transmission
  you wouldn't use. Switching storage later is a contained change if reads and writes go through one module, which is
  what I'd propose regardless.
